    {
      "id": "24p5va8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3. BALANCED AND UNBALANCED FORCES"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4ae2ogh",
      "content": "• Balanced Forces: Net force = 0 → No change in motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "43htlva",
      "content": "• Unbalanced Forces: Net force ≠ 0 → Acceleration produced."
    },
    {
      "id": "fhpfi11",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "4. INERTIA"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vw0b6v6",
      "content": "Inertia is the property of a body to resist change in its state of rest or motion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7slu47q",
      "content": "Mass is the measure of inertia."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j9l8pnm",
      "content": "Types of Inertia:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "nvjwuns",
      "content": "• Inertia of Rest"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "womh9fo",
      "content": "• Inertia of Motion"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "9y7xmk6",
      "content": "• Inertia of Direction"
    },
    {
      "id": "ueye0q0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "5. MOMENTUM"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l7c4eb2",
      "content": "Momentum (p) = Mass × Velocity"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "808z18w",
      "content": "p = m × v"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "e2ldns4",
      "content": "SI Unit = kg m/s"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ahtoakv",
      "content": "Momentum is a vector quantity."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "71qapgv",
      "content": "Dimension of Momentum = [M¹L¹T⁻¹]"
    },
    {
      "id": "mc0r8nj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "6. IMPULSE"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "3ohpt6a",
      "content": "Impulse = Force × Time"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "iw3tj65",
      "content": "Impulse = Change in Momentum"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "e8ux1za",
      "content": "SI Unit = Newton-second (Ns)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "owekkbf",
      "content": "Example: Pulling hands backward while catching a cricket ball reduces force."
    },
    {
      "id": "0bmlypq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "7. NEWTON’S LAWS OF MOTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4mig40b",
      "content": "First Law (Law of Inertia):"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "et66t87",
      "content": "A body remains at rest or in uniform motion unless acted upon by external force.<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pblt498",
      "content": "Second Law:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6ml8qr9",
      "content": "Rate of change of momentum is directly proportional to applied force."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gzb5qwf",
      "content": "F = ma"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "opb3ird",
      "content": "Third Law:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8x2t3yp",
      "content": "For every action, there is equal and opposite reaction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ra0ziqz",
      "content": "Action and reaction act on different bodies."
    },
    {
      "id": "k6mi7t7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "8. LAW OF CONSERVATION OF MOMENTUM"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u7cb48q",
      "content": "In absence of external force, total momentum before collision = total momentum after collision."
    },
    {
      "id": "0j1baot",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "9. ROCKET PROPULSION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j2jbzts",
      "content": "Rocket works on Newton’s Third Law and Conservation of Momentum."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pq9s73p",
      "content": "Gases expelled backward → Rocket moves forward."
    },
    {
      "id": "4enn5dd",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "10. FRICTION"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5p7rvco",
      "content": "Friction is the force opposing relative motion between surfaces in contact."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ky29d1q",
      "content": "Types: Static, Sliding, Rolling, Fluid friction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xc720vc",
      "content": "Advantages: Walking, writing, braking."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uy81mds",
      "content": "Disadvantages: Heat production, wear and tear."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aadvwas",
      "content": "Ways to Reduce Friction:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0ndxadd",
      "content": "• Use lubricants"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "uwuopvo",
      "content": "• Use ball bearings"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "87qeglb",
      "content": "• Polishing surfaces"
    },
    {
      "id": "1nph252",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "11. PRESSURE"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "l2q3dm2",
      "content": "Pressure = Force / Area"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "d0k9gow",
      "content": "SI Unit = Pascal (Pa)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "pvmqvq5",
      "content": "1 Pascal = 1 N/m²"
    },
    {
      "id": "4sma9zc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "12. FRAME OF REFERENCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "48lykso",
      "content": "A frame of reference is a coordinate system with respect to which motion is measured. There are two types of frames: Inertial and Non-Inertial."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pl2qvcu",
      "content": "• Inertial Frame: Newton’s laws are valid (no acceleration of frame)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wphg0jo",
      "content": "• Non-Inertial Frame: Frame is accelerating; pseudo force appears."
    },
    {
      "id": "yg481p4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "13. PSEUDO FORCE (DETAILED)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ftzuv4f",
      "content": "Pseudo force is an apparent force observed in a non-inertial frame. It does not arise due to physical interaction but due to acceleration of the frame."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8t8p9m8",
      "content": "Formula: F_pseudo = m × a_frame"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "odlmsi8",
      "content": "Example: Feeling pushed backward in an accelerating bus."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "1oosmnj",
      "content": "Direction: Opposite to acceleration of the frame."
    },
    {
      "id": "hd7t2jg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "14. FREE BODY DIAGRAM (FBD)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uoqorjn",
      "content": "A Free Body Diagram shows all forces acting on a body separately. It is essential for solving Newton’s Law numericals."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2l7ckuk",
      "content": "Steps to Draw FBD:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "csioo25",
      "content": "1. Isolate the object from surroundings."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "plwps1o",
      "content": "2. Represent object as a dot or box."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hbopdml",
      "content": "3. Draw all external forces (Weight, Normal, Friction, Tension, Applied Force)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ugvbqr1",
      "content": "4. Mark direction clearly."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5okycp5",
      "content": "5. Apply ∑F = ma."
    },
    {
      "id": "xd64093",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "15. EQUILIBRIUM OF A BODY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "amzfqap",
      "content": "A body is said to be in equilibrium when the net external force acting on it is zero."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ptnm48w",
      "content": "Mathematical Condition: ∑F = 0"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0ot1nl9",
      "content": "If ∑F = 0 → Acceleration = 0"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3cwb1py",
      "content": "Types of Equilibrium:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rlw4d9x",
      "content": "• Static Equilibrium (Body at rest)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sh9sot2",
      "content": "• Dynamic Equilibrium (Body in uniform motion)"
    },
    {
      "id": "uetqb05",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "16. MASS VS WEIGHT (DETAILED COMPARISON)"
    },
    {
      "id": "ml7gmla",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>",
